+## TL;DR
+- `openai/whisper`, `SYSTRAN/faster-whisper`, and `m-bain/whisperX` are the strongest open source stack for podcast transcription, alignment, and speaker-aware transcripts.
+- `Rikorose/DeepFilterNet`, `resemble-ai/resemble-enhance`, and `adefossez/demucs` cover the most common podcaster cleanup jobs: noise removal, speech enhancement, and music or stem separation.
+- `coqui-ai/TTS`, `myshell-ai/OpenVoice`, `WhisperSpeech/WhisperSpeech`, and `OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl` are the most practical GitHub-native options for local voice generation and dubbing.
+- `pyannote/pyannote-audio`, `snakers4/silero-vad`, and `wenet-e2e/wespeaker` help turn raw interviews into structured, speaker-aware production assets.
+- Direct GitHub searches for "podcaster" repos were weak, so this list broadens honestly into adjacent open source speech and audio AI tools that fit real podcast workflows.
+
+## Why This List
+Most "AI for podcasting" roundups are dominated by hosted products, pricing pages, and affiliate links. This list instead tracks the best AI tools for podcasters in 2026 that you can inspect, fork, self-host, or integrate into your own workflow from public GitHub repositories. That matters if you care about privacy, repeatable pipelines, local processing, or building podcast automation on top of stable open source components.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+Metadata below was checked against GitHub in April 2026. Stars, language, license, and last-activity dates come from visible GitHub repository, org, activity, or release pages; where GitHub surfaced activity inconsistently in search, dates are normalized from the latest visible public activity page.
